%% Chapter 34: Lexer Construction
\label{ch:lexer-construction}

\section{Introduction to Lexical Analysis}
\label{sec:lexer-intro}

Lexical analysis (or \emph{lexing} or \emph{scanning}) is the first phase of compilation. The lexer reads the source text as a stream of characters and groups them into meaningful units called \emph{tokens}. Each token has a \emph{type} (e.g., identifier, keyword, number, operator) and optionally a \emph{value} (e.g., the actual text of an identifier or the numeric value of a literal).

The theory of lexical analysis rests on regular languages and finite automata. Every token pattern can be described by a regular expression, and the lexer itself is a finite automaton that recognizes these patterns. The construction of a lexer from a set of regular expressions involves three steps:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Convert each regular expression to an NFA (Thompson's construction)
  \item Combine all NFAs into a single NFA (with a new start state and $\epsilon$-transitions)
  \item Convert the combined NFA to a DFA (subset construction)
  \item Minimize the DFA (Hopcroft's algorithm)
\end{enumerate}

\begin{historical}
The first lexer generator, \texttt{lex}, was written by Mike Lesk and Eric Schmidt at Bell Labs in 1975. It was distributed with Unix and became the standard tool for generating lexical analyzers. Later tools include \texttt{flex} (fast lex, by Vern Paxson, 1987), which generates significantly faster scanners, and \texttt{re2c} (by Dmitry Kurochkin, 1993), which generates C code optimized for speed. Modern alternatives include lexer generators integrated into parser generators (ANTLR, JavaCC) and library-based approaches (lexer combinators in functional languages).
\end{historical}

\section{From Regular Expressions to NFAs}
\label{sec:regex-to-nfa}

\subsection{Thompson's Construction}
\label{sec:thompson-construction}

Thompson's construction (Ken Thompson, 1968) converts a regular expression to an NFA with $\epsilon$-transitions. The construction is recursive, building small NFAs for each subexpression and combining them.

\begin{definition}[Thompson's Construction]
\label{def:thompson-construction}
For a regular expression $r$, the NFA $N(r)$ is constructed as follows:

\textbf{Base cases:}
\begin{itemize}
  \item $N(\epsilon)$: two states $s_0 \xrightarrow{\epsilon} s_1$ where $s_0$ is start and $s_1$ is accept
  \item $N(a)$ for $a \in \Sigma$: two states $s_0 \xrightarrow{a} s_1$ where $s_0$ is start and $s_1$ is accept
  \item $N(\emptyset)$: a single non-accepting state
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Recursive cases:}
\begin{itemize}
  \item $N(r_1 \mid r_2)$: new start state $s$ with $\epsilon$-transitions to start states of $N(r_1)$ and $N(r_2)$; accept states of $N(r_1)$ and $N(r_2)$ have $\epsilon$-transitions to new accept state $f$
  \item $N(r_1 \cdot r_2)$: accept states of $N(r_1)$ connected by $\epsilon$-transitions to start state of $N(r_2)$
  \item $N(r^*)$: new start/accept state $s$ with $\epsilon$-transitions to start of $N(r)$ and from accept of $N(r)$ back to start of $N(r)$, and from accept of $N(r)$ to $s$
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Thompson's Construction for $(a \mid b)^* a$]
\label{ex:thompson}
The regular expression $(a \mid b)^* a$ produces an NFA with the following structure:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Start state $s_0$ connects via $\epsilon$ to the start of $(a \mid b)^*$
  \item $(a \mid b)^*$ has a loop structure with $\epsilon$-transitions
  \item The accept state of $(a \mid b)^*$ connects via $\epsilon$ to the start of $a$
  \item The accept state of $a$ is the overall accept state
\end{itemize}
The resulting NFA has 6 states and 8 transitions.
\end{example}

\subsection{Size of Thompson's Construction}
\label{sec:thompson-size}

\begin{lemma}[Size of Thompson NFA]
\label{lem:thompson-size}
The NFA produced by Thompson's construction for a regular expression of length $n$ has at most $2n$ states and at most $4n$ transitions.
\end{lemma}

This linear size bound is important for the efficiency of lexer generators.

\section{From NFAs to DFAs: Subset Construction}
\label{sec:subset-construction}

\subsection{The Subset Construction Algorithm}
\label{sec:subset-algorithm}

The subset construction (or powerset construction) converts an NFA to a DFA by tracking the set of possible states the NFA could be in at each point.

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Convert NFA $N = (Q_N, \Sigma, \delta_N, q_0, F_N)$ to DFA $D$}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $q_0' \gets \epsilon\text{-closure}(\{q_0\})$
\State $Q_D \gets \{q_0'\}$, $F_D \gets \emptyset$
\State $\texttt{worklist} \gets [q_0']$
\State $\texttt{unmarked} \gets \{q_0'\}$
\While{$\texttt{unmarked}$ is not empty}
  \State Remove a state $S$ from $\texttt{unmarked}$
  \If{$S \cap F_N \neq \emptyset$}
    \State $F_D \gets F_D \cup \{S\}$
  \EndIf
  \For{each $a \in \Sigma$}
    \State $S' \gets \epsilon\text{-closure}\left(\bigcup_{q \in S} \delta_N(q, a)\right)$
    \If{$S' \notin Q_D$}
      \State $Q_D \gets Q_D \cup \{S'\}$
      \State Add $S'$ to $\texttt{unmarked}$
    \EndIf
    \State $\delta_D(S, a) \gets S'$
  \EndFor
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{$\epsilon$-Closure}
\label{sec:epsilon-closure}

The $\epsilon$-closure of a set of states $S$ is the set of all states reachable from $S$ by following zero or more $\epsilon$-transitions:

\[
\epsilon\text{-closure}(S) = S \cup \{q' \mid \exists q \in S.\; q \xrightarrow{\epsilon}^* q'\}
\]

This can be computed by a simple graph traversal (BFS or DFS) on the $\epsilon$-transition graph.

\subsection{Complexity}
\label{sec:subset-complexity}

\begin{theorem}[Complexity of Subset Construction]
\label{thm:subset-complexity}
Given an NFA with $n$ states and an alphabet of size $k$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Time complexity: $O(2^n \cdot k)$ in the worst case
  \item Space complexity: $O(2^n)$ for the DFA states
\end{itemize}
In practice, the number of reachable DFA states is often much smaller than $2^n$.
\end{theorem}

\section{DFA Minimization: Hopcroft's Algorithm}
\label{sec:hopcroft-algorithm}

\subsection{Motivation}
\label{sec:minimization-motivation}

The DFA produced by the subset construction may have many redundant states---states that behave identically for all inputs. Minimization produces the smallest equivalent DFA, which is unique up to renaming of states.

\subsection{The Algorithm}
\label{sec:hopcroft-detail}

Hopcroft's algorithm works by iteratively refining a partition of the states:

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Minimize DFA $D = (Q, \Sigma, \delta, q_0, F)$}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Remove unreachable states from $D$
\State $P \gets \{F, Q \setminus F\}$ \Comment{Initial partition}
\State $W \gets \{F, Q \setminus F\}$ \Comment{Worklist}
\While{$W \neq \emptyset$}
  \State Remove a set $A$ from $W$
  \For{each $c \in \Sigma$}
    \State $X \gets \{q \in Q \mid \delta(q, c) \in A\}$
    \For{each set $Y$ in $P$ such that $Y \cap X \neq \emptyset$ and $Y \setminus X \neq \emptyset$}
      \State Replace $Y$ in $P$ with $Y \cap X$ and $Y \setminus X$
      \If{$(Y, c) \in W$}
        \State Replace $(Y, c)$ in $W$ with $(Y \cap X, c)$ and $(Y \setminus X, c)$
      \Else
        \State Add the smaller of $(Y \cap X, c)$ and $(Y \setminus X, c)$ to $W$
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
  \EndFor
\EndWhile
\State Build minimized DFA from partition $P$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}[Complexity of Hopcroft's Algorithm]
\label{thm:hopcroft-complexity}
Hopcroft's algorithm runs in $O(n \log n)$ time, where $n$ is the number of states in the DFA. This is optimal in the worst case.
\end{theorem}

\section{Lexer Generators}
\label{sec:lexer-generators}

\subsection{Lex and Flex}
\label{sec:lex-flex}

\texttt{lex} and its successor \texttt{flex} are the most widely used lexer generators. A \texttt{lex} input file has three sections separated by \texttt{\%\%}:

\begin{verbatim}
  %{
  /* C code: declarations and includes */
  %}
  
  /* Definitions: macros and start conditions */
  digit    [0-9]
  letter   [a-zA-Z]
  
  %%
  /* Rules: regular expressions and actions */
  {digit}+          { yylval = atoi(yytext); return NUMBER; }
  {letter}({letter}|{digit})*  { yylval = strdup(yytext); return ID; }
  [ \t\n]+          ; /* skip whitespace */
  "//".*            ; /* skip comments */
  "+"               { return PLUS; }
  "*"               { return TIMES; }
  .                 { return ERROR; }
  %%
  
  /* C code: auxiliary functions */
\end{verbatim}

\texttt{flex} generates a C function \texttt{yylex()} that, when called, reads the next token from the input and returns its token type.

\subsection{re2c}
\label{sec:re2c}

\texttt{re2c} is a lexer generator that produces highly optimized C code. Unlike \texttt{flex}, which generates a table-driven scanner, \texttt{re2c} generates inline code with explicit state transitions, resulting in faster execution.

\begin{verbatim}
  /*!re2c
    re2c:define:YYCTYPE = char;
    re2c:define:YYCURSOR = cursor;
    
    digit = [0-9];
    number = digit+;
    
    number { return NUMBER; }
    [ \t\n]+ { continue; }
    *      { return ERROR; }
  */
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{ANTLR Lexer Rules}
\label{sec:antlr-lexer}

ANTLR integrates lexer and parser generation. Lexer rules are written in a syntax similar to EBNF:

\begin{verbatim}
  lexer grammar SimpleLexer;
  
  INT    : [0-9]+ ;
  ID     : [a-zA-Z] [a-zA-Z0-9]* ;
  WS     : [ \t\r\n]+ -> skip ;
  COMMENT: '//' ~[\r\n]* -> skip ;
\end{verbatim}

\section{Longest-Match and Priority Rules}
\label{sec:longest-match}

\subsection{The Longest-Match Rule}
\label{sec:longest-match-rule}

When multiple token patterns match at a given position, the lexer must choose one. The standard rule is:

\begin{definition}[Longest Match]
\label{def:longest-match}
The lexer returns the token that matches the \textbf{longest} prefix of the remaining input. If two patterns match the same length, the one listed first (or with higher priority) wins.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Longest Match]
\label{ex:longest-match}
Given the patterns:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{if} (keyword)
  \item \texttt{[a-z]+} (identifier)
\end{itemize}
On the input \texttt{iffy}, the identifier pattern matches all 4 characters while the keyword matches only 2. The longest-match rule selects the identifier \texttt{iffy}.

On the input \texttt{if}, both patterns match 2 characters. The keyword pattern wins because it has higher priority (listed first).
\end{example}

\subsection{Implementing Longest Match}
\label{sec:implementing-longest-match}

Longest match is implemented by tracking the last accept state encountered during DFA simulation:

\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $\texttt{last\_accept} \gets \texttt{null}$
\State $\texttt{state} \gets q_0$
\State $\texttt{pos} \gets 0$
\While{$\texttt{state} \neq \texttt{dead}$ and $\texttt{pos} < \texttt{input.length}$}
  \If{$\texttt{state} \in F$}
    \State $\texttt{last\_accept} \gets (\texttt{state}, \texttt{pos})$
  \EndIf
  \State $\texttt{state} \gets \delta(\texttt{state}, \texttt{input}[\texttt{pos}])$
  \State $\texttt{pos} \gets \texttt{pos} + 1$
\EndWhile
\State \textbf{return} $\texttt{last\_accept}$
\end{algorithmic}

When the DFA reaches a dead state (no transition for the current character), the lexer backs up to the last accept state and returns the corresponding token.

\section{Unicode Lexer Challenges}
\label{sec:unicode-challenges}

\subsection{Unicode Character Classes}
\label{sec:unicode-classes}

Modern programming languages must handle Unicode source text. This introduces several challenges:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Character classes}: Unicode defines thousands of characters organized into categories (letters, digits, symbols, etc.). Regular expressions must support Unicode character classes like \texttt{\textbackslash p\{Letter\}}.
  \item \textbf{Normalization}: The same visual character can be represented by different byte sequences (e.g., composed vs.\ decomposed forms). Lexers must handle normalization.
  \item \textbf{Case folding}: Unicode case folding is complex---some characters have multiple case-folded forms.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Encoding Considerations}
\label{sec:encoding}

Source text may be encoded in UTF-8, UTF-16, or UTF-32. Each encoding has implications for lexer implementation:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{UTF-8}: Variable-length (1--4 bytes per character). Lexers must handle multi-byte sequences.
  \item \textbf{UTF-16}: Variable-length (1--2 code units). Surrogate pairs complicate pattern matching.
  \item \textbf{UTF-32}: Fixed-width (4 bytes per character). Simplest for lexing but uses more memory.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Unicode-Aware Lexer Design}
\label{sec:unicode-design}

Modern lexer generators handle Unicode by:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Decoding the input to code points before lexing
  \item Using Unicode-aware character classes
  \item Supporting case-insensitive matching with Unicode semantics
  \item Handling BOM (byte order mark) at the start of files
\end{itemize}

\section{Practical Considerations}
\label{sec:lexer-practical}

\subsection{Buffering and Input Handling}
\label{sec:buffering}

Lexers typically read input in blocks (buffers) rather than character by character. The buffer management must handle:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Token boundaries that span buffer boundaries
  \item Efficient backtracking for longest-match
  \item Memory-efficient handling of large input files
\end{itemize}

\texttt{flex} uses a two-buffer scheme: when the lexer reaches the end of one buffer, it reads the next block into the other buffer, ensuring that tokens spanning the boundary are handled correctly.

\subsection{Start Conditions}
\label{sec:start-conditions}

Start conditions (or \emph{lexer states}) allow the lexer to change its behavior based on context. For example, C comments require a different set of rules than normal code:

\begin{verbatim}
  %x COMMENT
  %%
  "/*"         { BEGIN(COMMENT); }
  <COMMENT>"*/" { BEGIN(INITIAL); }
  <COMMENT>.    ; /* ignore everything in comment */
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Performance Optimization}
\label{sec:lexer-performance}

Techniques for optimizing lexer performance include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Table compression}: Reduce the size of the transition table using default entries, compressed row representations, or hash tables.
  \item \textbf{Direct coding}: Generate switch statements or goto-based code instead of table lookups (used by \texttt{re2c}).
  \item \textbf{Lazy DFA construction}: Construct DFA states on demand rather than all at once (used by some regex engines).
  \item \textbf{SIMD acceleration}: Use vector instructions to process multiple characters simultaneously.
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary}
\label{sec:lexer-summary}

Lexer construction is a well-understood and highly automated process. The pipeline from regular expressions to executable lexers involves:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Specifying token patterns as regular expressions
  \item Converting to NFAs (Thompson's construction)
  \item Converting to DFAs (subset construction)
  \item Minimizing the DFA (Hopcroft's algorithm)
  \item Generating code (table-driven or direct-coded)
\end{enumerate}

Modern lexer generators handle additional complexities including Unicode, start conditions, and longest-match semantics. Despite the availability of automated tools, understanding the underlying theory is essential for debugging lexer issues, optimizing performance, and designing token specifications for new languages.

\begin{historical}
The evolution of lexer generators from \texttt{lex} (1975) to modern tools reflects broader trends in compiler technology. Key milestones include: \texttt{lex} (Lesk, 1975); \texttt{flex} (Paxson, 1987); \texttt{re2c} (Kurochkin, 1993); ANTLR's integrated lexer/parser (Parr, 1990s--present); and the development of Unicode-aware lexing. The theoretical foundations---Thompson's construction, the subset construction, and Hopcroft's algorithm---remain essentially unchanged since the 1960s and 1970s.
\end{historical}

