\section{Unicode Lexer Construction in Detail}
\label{sec:unicode-lexer-detail}

\subsection{Unicode Normalization and Lexing}
\label{sec:unicode-normalization}

The same visual character can be represented by different byte sequences in Unicode. For example, the character ``é'' can be encoded as:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{NFC} (Normalization Form C): A single code point U+00E9 (composed form)
  \item \textbf{NFD} (Normalization Form D): Two code points U+0065 U+0301 (decomposed: ``e'' + combining acute accent)
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[Unicode Normalization for Lexing]
\label{def:unicode-norm-lex}
A \textbf{Unicode-aware lexer} must normalize input to a canonical form before matching. The standard approach is to normalize to NFC before lexing, ensuring that equivalent characters are treated identically.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Normalization in Identifiers]
\label{ex:norm-identifiers}
Consider a lexer rule for identifiers: $\texttt{[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9]*}$. Without normalization:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{café} (NFC, 4 code points) matches the identifier pattern
  \item \texttt{café} (NFD, 5 code points) also matches, but the combining accent is treated as a separate character
\end{itemize}
Both should produce the same identifier. Normalization ensures consistent treatment.
\end{example}

\subsection{Unicode Character Properties}
\label{sec:unicode-properties}

Unicode defines character properties that can be used in lexer specifications:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{p{4cm}p{8cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{Description} \\
\hline
\texttt{L} (Letter) & All letters (uppercase, lowercase, titlecase, modifier, other) \\
\texttt{Lu} & Uppercase letters \\
\texttt{Ll} & Lowercase letters \\
\texttt{Nd} & Decimal digit numbers \\
\texttt{Zs} & Space separator characters \\
\texttt{Zl} & Line separator \\
\texttt{Zp} & Paragraph separator \\
\texttt{P} & Punctuation (all categories) \\
\texttt{XID\_Start} & Characters valid at the start of an identifier \\
\texttt{XID\_Continue} & Characters valid in the continuation of an identifier \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Common Unicode character properties for lexing}
\label{tab:unicode-properties}
\end{table}

\begin{example}[Unicode Identifier Rules]
\label{ex:unicode-ident}
The Unicode Standard Annex \#31 recommends identifier rules based on character properties:
\begin{verbatim}
  identifier_start = [\p{XID_Start}]
  identifier_continue = [\p{XID_Continue}]
  identifier = identifier_start identifier_continue*
\end{verbatim}
This allows identifiers to contain letters, digits, and certain combining marks from any script.
\end{example}

\subsection{Handling Surrogate Pairs in UTF-16}
\label{sec:surrogate-pairs}

UTF-16 encodes characters above U+FFFF as \emph{surrogate pairs}---two 16-bit code units. This affects lexer construction:

\begin{definition}[Surrogate Pair]
\label{def:surrogate-pair}
A \textbf{surrogate pair} consists of a high surrogate (U+D800--U+DBFF) followed by a low surrogate (U+DC00--U+DFFF), encoding a single code point above U+FFFF.
\end{definition}

\begin{insight}
When building a DFA for UTF-16 encoded text, surrogate pairs must be handled correctly. A character class like \texttt{[\textbackslash p\{Emoji\}]} may include characters above U+FFFF, which are encoded as surrogate pairs in UTF-16. The DFA must match the \emph{pair} of code units, not individual code units.
\end{insight}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Match a Unicode character property in UTF-16}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Read code unit $c_1$
\If{$c_1$ is a high surrogate (U+D800--U+DBFF)}
  \State Read code unit $c_2$
  \If{$c_2$ is a low surrogate (U+DC00--U+DFFF)}
    \State $cp \gets (c_1 - \text{0xD800}) \times \text{0x400} + (c_2 - \text{0xDC00}) + \text{0x10000}$
    \State Check if $cp$ satisfies the character property
  \Else
    \State Error: lone high surrogate
  \EndIf
\Else
  \State $cp \gets c_1$
  \State Check if $cp$ satisfies the character property
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Practical Lexer Issues}
\label{sec:practical-lexer-issues}

\subsection{Lexer--Parser Interaction}
\label{sec:lexer-parser-interaction}

The lexer and parser are not independent: the parser's state can influence the lexer's behavior. Common patterns include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Context-sensitive keywords}: In some languages, certain words are keywords only in certain contexts. For example, in C++, \texttt{<<} is a left-shift operator or a stream insertion operator depending on context.
  \item \textbf{Whitespace significance}: In Python, indentation is significant. The lexer must track indentation levels and emit INDENT/DEDENT tokens.
  \item \textbf{Lexer modes}: The lexer switches between different modes (e.g., normal code vs.\ string literals vs.\ regular expressions).
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Python Indentation Handling]
\label{ex:python-indent}
Python's lexer maintains a stack of indentation levels:
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $\text{indent\_stack} \gets [0]$
\For{each line}
  \State $\text{indent} \gets \text{leading whitespace of line}$
  \If{$\text{indent} > \text{indent\_stack.top}$}
    \State Push $\text{indent}$ onto $\text{indent\_stack}$
    \State Emit INDENT token
  \ElsIf{$\text{indent} < \text{indent\_stack.top}$}
    \While{$\text{indent} < \text{indent\_stack.top}$}
      \State Pop $\text{indent\_stack}$
      \State Emit DEDENT token
    \EndWhile
    \If{$\text{indent} \neq \text{indent\_stack.top}$}
      \State Error: inconsistent indentation
    \EndIf
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State Emit DEDENT for each remaining level on the stack
\end{algorithmic}
\end{example}

\subsection{Handling Comments and String Literals}
\label{sec:comments-strings}

Comments and string literals require special handling because their contents are not subject to normal lexing rules:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Block comments}: In C (\texttt{/* ... */}), the lexer must track nesting (or not, for C) and ignore all content within the comment.
  \item \textbf{String escape sequences}: In most languages, \texttt{\textbackslash n}, \texttt{\textbackslash t}, \texttt{\textbackslash\textbackslash}, etc.\ within strings must be processed by the lexer.
  \item \textbf{Raw strings}: Languages like Python (\texttt{r"..."}) and Rust (\texttt{r"..."}) have raw strings where escape sequences are not processed.
  \item \textbf{Nested delimiters}: Rust's raw strings use \texttt{r\#"..."\#} where the number of \texttt{\#} characters determines the nesting depth.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Rust Raw String Lexing]
\label{ex:rust-raw-string}
Rust raw strings \texttt{r\#\#"..."\#\#} require the lexer to:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Count the number of \texttt{\#} characters after \texttt{r}
  \item Scan until finding \texttt{"} followed by the same number of \texttt{\#} characters
  \item Handle the case where \texttt{"\#} appears inside the string (not a terminator)
\end{enumerate}
This is not a regular pattern---it requires counting. However, since the number of \texttt{\#} is bounded in practice, it can be handled with a finite number of lexer states.
\end{example}

\subsection{Incremental Lexing for IDEs}
\label{sec:incremental-lexing}

IDEs need to re-lex source code after each keystroke. Full re-lexing is too slow for large files. \emph{Incremental lexing} re-lexes only the affected portion:

\begin{definition}[Incremental Lexing]
\label{def:incremental-lexing}
\textbf{Incremental lexing} maintains a cache of token boundaries and their associated DFA states. When the source is edited:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify the token(s) affected by the edit.
  \item Re-lex from the start of the affected token.
  \item Stop when the DFA reaches the same state as the cached state at a subsequent token boundary.
  \item Reuse all subsequent cached tokens.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Incremental Lexing Efficiency]
\label{thm:incremental-lex}
For a local edit (changing a constant number of characters), incremental lexing takes $O(k)$ time where $k$ is the number of tokens affected by the change, compared to $O(n)$ for full re-lexing. In the common case, $k \ll n$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Error Handling in Lexers}
\label{sec:lexer-error-handling}

When the lexer encounters a character that does not match any token pattern, it must handle the error:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Report and skip}: Report an error and skip the offending character.
  \item \textbf{Report and consume}: Report an error and consume characters until a valid token start is found.
  \item \textbf{Insert a special token}: Return an ERROR token containing the unrecognized text, allowing the parser to handle the error.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Lexer Error Recovery]
\label{ex:lexer-error}
On encountering \texttt{@} in a language where \texttt{@} is not valid:
\begin{verbatim}
  Error at line 5, column 12: unexpected character '@'
  Skipping character and continuing...
\end{verbatim}
The lexer reports the error, skips \texttt{@}, and continues lexing from the next character.
\end{example}

\section{Lexer Testing and Verification}
\label{sec:lexer-testing}

\subsection{Property-Based Testing}
\label{sec:lexer-property-testing}

Lexer generators can be tested using property-based testing (e.g., QuickCheck):

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Round-trip property}: For every token $t$, the lexer produces $t$ when given the text of $t$ as input.
  \item \textbf{Completeness property}: Every character sequence is either lexed into a sequence of tokens or produces an error.
  \item \textbf{Determinism property}: The lexer produces the same output for the same input, regardless of buffer boundaries.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Formal Verification of Lexers}
\label{sec:lexer-verification}

The correctness of the lexer construction pipeline can be formally verified:

\begin{theorem}[Correctness of Thompson's Construction]
\label{thm:thompson-correct}
The NFA produced by Thompson's construction for a regular expression $r$ accepts exactly the language denoted by $r$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Correctness of Subset Construction]
\label{thm:subset-correct}
The DFA produced by the subset construction accepts exactly the same language as the input NFA.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Correctness of Hopcroft's Algorithm]
\label{thm:hopcroft-correct}
The DFA produced by Hopcroft's algorithm is the unique minimal DFA (up to state renaming) for the input language.
\end{theorem}

These theorems have been formally verified in proof assistants such as Coq and Isabelle/HOL, providing machine-checked guarantees of lexer correctness.

