\subsection{Finding a Shortest Token-Label Disagreement}

Testing a minimized lexer against its unminimized counterpart calls for more
than language equivalence. Two states can both accept a prefix but return
different tokens because the rules for that prefix had different priorities.
Compare the two automata as labelled machines: each state carries either the
winning rule at that prefix or a distinguished nonaccepting mark. Use a
common alphabet partition (the refinement of both machines' character
classes), complete missing moves with a nonaccepting sink, and choose one
representative character per class. Compare corresponding start states
separately for each lexer mode. If actions change mode, the label must
identify the action as well as the token kind.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Complete labelled DFAs $A,B$, representatives $C$
\State $r \gets (\mathrm{start}(A),\mathrm{start}(B))$
\State enqueue $r$; set $\mathrm{parent}[r] \gets \bot$
\While{queue is nonempty}
  \State dequeue $u=(p,q)$
  \If{$\mathrm{label}_A(p) \ne \mathrm{label}_B(q)$}
    \State \Return path to $u$ \Comment{Read parent letters in reverse}
  \EndIf
  \ForAll{$c \in C$}
    \State $v \gets (\delta_A(p,c),\delta_B(q,c))$
    \If{$v$ has no parent entry}
      \State $\mathrm{parent}[v] \gets (u,c)$ \Comment{First path is shortest}
      \State enqueue $v$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\EndWhile
\State \Return equivalent
\end{sudocode}

For example, take rules \texttt{IF = if} and \texttt{ID = [a-z]+}.
Put \texttt{IF} first in $A$ but second in $B$. After \texttt{i}, both
states carry \texttt{ID}; after an additional \texttt{f}, they carry
\texttt{IF} and \texttt{ID}, respectively. The queue returns \texttt{if}:
all one-letter prefixes had matching labels. On input ending after those
two letters, the difference is visible in the emitted token.

The invariant is that a queued pair represents the states reached by both
automata on the same reconstructed prefix. FIFO order measures distance in
characters, not bytes. At most $|A||B|$ pairs are stored, with one move per
character class considered per visited pair. Equality of prefix labels
preserves the choice of the last accepting prefix, hence the token and its
extent under longest match. Buffer refills and decoding still require
separate tests: this check starts with decoded characters, not raw bytes.
